{
  "dom_id": "step-q76094",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are injections?\n\nI. $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=2x-6$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to R$ defined by $h(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **injection** (also known as **one-to-one**) if *every* element in $x$ in $X$ is mapped to a *distinct* element in $Y.$\n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ if $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\n\n- I is *not* an injection. For example, $f(-2)=4=f(2),$ but $-2\\ne 2.$\n\n- II is an injection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-6 & =2{x}_{2}-6 \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- III is *not* an injection. For example, $h(\\pi )=0=h(2\\pi ),$ but $\\pi \\ne 2\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
